## Food

There is no special PCOS diet. A balanced diet with plenty of vegetables, wholegrains, beans and lean protein, and fewer sugary foods and drinks, can help your body manage insulin.

## Being active

Aim for at least 150 minutes of activity a week. Strength exercises can be especially helpful.

## Body image and mood

Changes to hair, skin and weight can affect how you feel about yourself. Anxiety and depression are more common in PCOS. Talk to your GP if you are struggling. You can refer yourself to **NHS Talking Therapies** in England.

## Health checks

Ask your GP about regular checks for **blood sugar**, blood pressure and cholesterol. See your GP if you have fewer than 3 or 4 periods a year, or any unusual bleeding.

## Support

**Verity** is a UK charity for people with PCOS.
